**Landed: 0.11.451** (srcversion `AA5317A2F0970AA56BAB4BF`), builds clean — warning set is byte-for-byte the pre-existing baseline, no new warnings.

This session implemented all four items on sess133's NOT-DONE list, i.e. the whole D2/D3/D4/D6 design the sess133 ruling approved:

**The lifecycle state machine (D2/D4).** `ctx->lc` is live and `stop_ran` is gone. `stop()` now elects exactly one teardown owner under `lreq_lock`; every other caller blocks for `STOPPED` and returns the owner's stored verdict. That closes the actual defect: a second caller used to read `running == false` mid-teardown and return as though teardown had *completed*, free to destroy a context the first caller was still inside — and `destroy()` makes exactly that call. Verdict-then-state-then-broadcast is in the ruling's required order, and both accessors now read under the lock.

**One correction to the plan I inherited.** The sess133 note said to tighten `caw_op_enter` to `lc == RUNNING`. That is wrong and would have broken every mount: `mxfs_dlm_caw_purge_dead_nodes_ex` is a gated entry point and runs at `v5_mount.c:3728`, between `create()` and `start()`. The landed predicate admits NEW/STARTING/RUNNING and refuses START_FAILED — still strictly tighter than the old `!ops_closed`.

**D6 is bounded and ends in fail-stop.** One *absolute* deadline for the whole teardown (155s escalation, then a clamped 30–60s grace) rather than a per-phase one, because phases 2, 3 and 5 all block and a bound that resets per phase isn't a bound. Phase 2 enforces the terminal deadline itself — the deferred escalation is a channel and may never run. All three teardown joins plus the failed-start unwind join went through `caw_join_bounded`.

**Also fixed:** `dlm/mount.c:2507`, the failed-mount path that stopped without `release_on_stop` and left its bits on the disk.

**One trap worth the note it got in memory:** `__noreturn` on a PAL function produces an objtool fall-through warning that neither `return;`, `unreachable()`, nor moving the call off the function tail suppresses. The fix was to drop the attribute and make `mxfs_pal_failstop` a macro whose fallback is a real spin — which is also the more honest guarantee.

**What I found and did not fix:** `mxfs_v5_dlm_set_dlm_stuck_notify` has no caller anywhere in the tree, so `dlm_stuck_notify_fn` is always NULL and the escalation chain dead-ends at v5. The ruling's "asynchronously request force-shutdown" does not actually reach XFS yet. That plus the RULE-5 re-consult and the `create()` unwind leak are the next session's queue.

Not production ready — 29 open defects, 17 critical. This session landed the largest piece of the D2/D3/D4/D6 work but closed nothing: none of it is rig-verified, and under RULE 6 those entries stay open until a test exercises the cause.

